    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-83179",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation",
      "problem": "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯,\\,x\\in (-1,1]\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =x \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}{x}^{1}}{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3}{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{4}{x}^{3}}{3} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{(-1)^{5}{x}^{4}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}.\n$$",
