      "problem": "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for ${3}^{x},$\n given by\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}=1+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)x+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{3}{x}^{3}}{3!}+⋯\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)x \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}{x}^{2}}{2!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{3}{x}^{3}}{3!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{0}{x}^{0}}{0!} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{1}{x}^{1}}{1!} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}{x}^{2}}{2!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{3}{x}^{3}}{3!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{n}{x}^{n}}{n!}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{n}{x}^{n}}{n!}\\,.\n$$",
