      "math_academy_id": "q-125339",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation",
      "problem": "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8}+⋯\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation.",
      "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{1+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 1}}{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 3}}{3} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{(-1)^{4+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 4}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n}}{n}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$",
